1
00:00:00,360 --> 00:00:05,780
Welcome to today's deep dive. We are analyzing the mechanics of digital public infrastructure.

2
00:00:06,300 --> 00:00:12,200
Uh, specifically, we're looking at how government sectors can avoid building fragmented, siloed systems.

3
00:00:12,700 --> 00:00:17,320
Right. Because nobody in your ministry wants to end up locked into one supplier or

4
00:00:18,020 --> 00:00:21,220
dealing with platforms that are just incredibly costly to change.

5
00:00:21,600 --> 00:00:22,880
Exactly. So

6
00:00:23,480 --> 00:00:33,420
our mission today is to give you a clear understanding of why creating a shared catalog of services is your ultimate defense against duplicated technology projects that simply refuse to

7
00:00:33,440 --> 00:00:33,940
work together.

8
00:00:34,440 --> 00:00:39,620
Because without that defense, you just end up with data constantly having to be rekeyed across departments.

9
00:00:39,900 --> 00:00:41,860
Yeah, that is a huge drain on resources.

10
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:48,780
So let's say a donor comes to your program, and they offer to fund a brand-new digital licensing system for a quality authority.

11
00:00:49,200 --> 00:00:52,480
I mean, the instinct is to just take the money immediately, right?

12
00:00:53,540 --> 00:00:58,100
Why must you pause and ask that donor for one page before saying yes?

13
00:00:58,840 --> 00:01:03,080
Well, you pause because that single page is the sector's catalog of services.

14
00:01:03,880 --> 00:01:05,300
It's written once for the whole sector,

15
00:01:05,920 --> 00:01:08,500
and it lists every single service owed to the public.

16
00:01:08,980 --> 00:01:12,620
Okay, so it's a comprehensive list, but what actually goes on it?

17
00:01:13,180 --> 00:01:18,160
Right. So every row in this catalog has to explicitly number and define four things.

18
00:01:18,800 --> 00:01:22,380
Uh, number one, the service. Number two, the body that owes it.

19
00:01:22,840 --> 00:01:24,560
Number three, how it runs today.

20
00:01:25,180 --> 00:01:28,220
And number four, the shared blocks it cannot run without.

21
00:01:28,740 --> 00:01:33,480
Wait, so by looking at number four, you're identifying those shared dependencies right up front.

22
00:01:33,900 --> 00:01:34,360
Precisely.

23
00:01:35,120 --> 00:01:38,780
To see why that matters, let's look at a fictional country called Progressa.

24
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:43,420
Okay, Progressa. Yes. Specifically, let's look at their higher education sector.

25
00:01:44,120 --> 00:01:50,840
Right. So in Progressa, you have several different bodies, uh, like PHEQA, the quality authority.

26
00:01:51,180 --> 00:01:52,820
Then there's MoEYS, the ministry,

27
00:01:53,420 --> 00:01:58,480
and PDCA for credentials. And collectively, they owe the public seven distinct services.

28
00:01:58,960 --> 00:02:02,700
And right now, those services probably just run on paper or spreadsheets.

29
00:02:03,500 --> 00:02:08,340
So when Progressa decides to digitize, you have to run the true infrastructure test.

30
00:02:09,039 --> 00:02:12,040
You do that by reading down one column of that catalog.

31
00:02:12,680 --> 00:02:14,020
Reading down the column.

32
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:16,360
So what does that actually show you?

33
00:02:16,980 --> 00:02:20,740
Well, let's say you read down the column for a national identity sign-in system.

34
00:02:21,360 --> 00:02:26,300
You quickly realize that six out of those seven higher education services rest entirely on it.

35
00:02:26,880 --> 00:02:29,420
Oh, wow, six out of seven. So almost everything needs it.

36
00:02:29,640 --> 00:02:36,040
Yeah, exactly. Which brings us to a key rule: a block with only one service in its column is just a project's tool.

37
00:02:36,380 --> 00:02:41,540
Right. But a block with many services resting on it, well, that is the sector's infrastructure.

38
00:02:42,300 --> 00:02:49,540
I hear what you're saying about infrastructure, but practically speaking, isn't it inherently faster for a single project in your ministry to just

39
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:54,880
build its own independent list of institutions rather than waiting to agree on a shared one?

40
00:02:55,300 --> 00:02:59,120
I mean, yes, it is faster inside one isolated project.

41
00:02:59,860 --> 00:03:05,500
But across the broader sector, it means your government ends up paying to build and host seven different lists.

42
00:03:05,960 --> 00:03:07,300
Ah, right. So it's duplicated.

43
00:03:07,560 --> 00:03:10,000
Exactly. And you spend years trying to make them agree.

44
00:03:11,000 --> 00:03:16,920
Which brings us to how the United Nations Development Program defines digital public infrastructure, or DPI.

45
00:03:17,380 --> 00:03:21,600
Right. They define DPI as foundational societal scale systems.

46
00:03:21,920 --> 00:03:26,680
Yeah. We're talking about things like digital identity, payments, and consent-based data sharing.

47
00:03:27,240 --> 00:03:31,360
And there is a really important warning about this in the reference architecture from GovStack, isn't there?

48
00:03:31,700 --> 00:03:32,160
There is.

49
00:03:32,920 --> 00:03:37,180
The reference architecture states very clearly that DPI is not neutral.

50
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:40,020
It actively shapes what can be built on top of it.

51
00:03:40,600 --> 00:03:44,520
So before you approve the very first digital project, you need to ask for the catalog.

52
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:48,660
Look down the columns to identify what must be funded for the whole sector.

